---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'What to Do If Your Employer Won\'t Pay Overtime | overtimechecker.com';
const description = 'Step-by-step on what to do when your employer fails to pay overtime: documentation, internal resolution, filing a wage claim, and when to hire an attorney.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>What to Do If Your Employer Won't Pay Overtime</h1>
  <p>If your employer isn't paying overtime you're owed, you have real options. Most of them are free or low-cost. Here's the playbook.</p>

  <h2>Step 1: Document everything</h2>
  <p>Start a personal record of your hours. Keep:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Daily start and end times</li>
    <li>Meal break length (and whether you actually got it)</li>
    <li>Off-the-clock work (emails answered, calls taken, prep work)</li>
    <li>Your pay stubs &mdash; save every one</li>
    <li>Communications about your hours (emails, texts, schedules)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do this BEFORE you raise the issue. If documentation surfaces after a complaint, employers can claim they wouldn't have kept inconsistent records.</p>

  <h2>Step 2: Calculate what you're owed</h2>
  <p>Use our <a href="/">overtime calculator</a> to estimate. Be conservative &mdash; calculate based on hours you can prove. Going back through your records, look at every workweek where you worked over 40 hours (or over your state's daily threshold).</p>

  <h2>Step 3: Try internal resolution (if it's safe)</h2>
  <p>Sometimes underpayment is a payroll error. A polite written request to HR or payroll, with your documented hours, can resolve it. Get any response in writing.</p>
  <p>Don't do this if you fear retaliation &mdash; while retaliation is illegal, prevention is better than enforcement.</p>

  <h2>Step 4: File a wage claim (free)</h2>
  <p>Two options:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>US Department of Labor's Wage and Hour Division</strong> (dol.gov/whd) handles federal FLSA claims. Filing is free; you don't need an attorney. WHD investigates and can recover back wages plus liquidated damages.</li>
    <li><strong>Your state's labor agency</strong> handles state-specific claims. State recovery is sometimes faster than federal for amounts within state limits.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You don't have to choose &mdash; you can file with both. Just don't accept duplicate recovery if both succeed.</p>

  <h2>Step 5: Consider an attorney for larger amounts</h2>
  <p>For unpaid overtime over a few thousand dollars, an attorney can often recover more than agency claims (especially with attorney's fees provisions in federal and most state laws). Many wage-and-hour attorneys work on contingency &mdash; no fee unless you recover.</p>
  <p>Many offer free consultations. Even one call can tell you whether your case is worth pursuing privately.</p>

  <h2>What if I'm afraid of retaliation?</h2>
  <p>Retaliation for wage complaints is illegal under FLSA and most state laws. If you're fired, demoted, or punished after raising a wage issue, that's a separate violation with its own remedies (often including back pay PLUS additional damages).</p>
  <p>Practical step: keep records of your performance and any reviews BEFORE complaining, so retaliation timing is easy to establish.</p>

  <h2>Statute of limitations</h2>
  <p>Federal: 2 years for non-willful violations, 3 years for willful. State limits vary; some allow longer. Don't wait.</p>
</BaseLayout>
